;; What a turn is told when its question mentions @here (byj-gly.453): what
;; the person is looking at in a running editor of the project's, read from
;; it as the turn begins (emacs/context! for the Emacs :emacs-server names,
;; else terminal/context!, RFC-067), so
;; "this" is never left for the model to guess or to remember to ask about.
;; Added to the turn's user-context zone, as the memory hint is. Read by the
;; model, so a resource (RFC-001). The session tree's :session/editor-context
;; node says which word is the mention.
;;
;; {program} the editor's name in the Terminal view; {where} the :file or
;; :buffer line; {line} {column}; {modified} the :modified words or nothing;
;; {org} in an org file the :org-heading and :org-block lines, each with
;; its own leading newline, or nothing (RFC-067);
;; {region} the :region or :no-region words; {text} the selection; {why} what
;; kept the editor from saying.
{:context
 "## @here — what the person is looking at

When they wrote @here they meant this, in {program}:
{where}
- Cursor at line {line}, column {column}{modified}{org}

{region}"
 :file "- File: `{file}`"
 :buffer "- Buffer: `{buffer}` (visiting no file)"
 :modified " — with unsaved edits, so the file on disk is not what they see"
 :org-heading "\n- Under the org heading: {heading}"
 :org-block "\n- In the{name} src block of {language} that starts at line {line}"
 :region "They have selected:
```
{text}
```"
 :no-region "Nothing is selected: they mean the code around that line. Read the file there before answering."
 ;; @visible and @trail (RFC-067 phase 10): Emacs only. {rows} the lines
 ;; below, one a place; {mention} the word; {why} what kept Emacs from saying.
 :visible
 "## @visible — what is on the person's screen

When they wrote @visible they meant the windows in front of them:
{rows}"
 :visible-row "- `{where}`, lines {start}–{end}, cursor at line {line}{selected}"
 :selected " — the window they are in"
 :trail
 "## @trail — where the person has been, most recent first

When they wrote @trail they meant the files they have just been reading, which say what they are looking into:
{rows}"
 :trail-row "- `{file}` at line {line}{ago}"
 :ago ", {minutes} min ago"
 :places-none
 "## {mention}

The person wrote {mention}, meaning their Emacs's windows or the files they visited, but it could not be read ({why}). Ask them rather than guessing."
 :none
 "## @here

The person wrote @here, meaning what they are looking at in their editor, but it could not be read ({why}). Ask them which file and lines they mean rather than guessing."}
